<h2>A two-decade journey through three courts</h2>
<p>The appellant was acquitted by the trial court of criminal breach of trust under Section 409 IPC. In 2017, the Bombay High Court (Aurangabad Bench) reversed that acquittal, relying centrally on documents said to have been seized from his house. The Supreme Court&rsquo;s judgment of 8 October 2026 — in an appeal instituted in 2019 — completes the arc, restoring the acquittal and cancelling the bail bonds.</p>

<h2>The seizure that could not be proved</h2>
<p>The prosecution&rsquo;s case depended on 46 articles allegedly seized from the appellant&rsquo;s house. The seizure, however, failed every test of proof. Both panch witnesses turned hostile. The accused&rsquo;s signature was absent from the seizure panchnama — a gap the High Court had dismissed as insignificant. No list of the seized items was supplied to the accused, contrary to Section 100(7) CrPC, and the attestation safeguards of Section 100(4) went unobserved. The Supreme Court held that in these circumstances, &ldquo;the seizure could not be held to be duly proved solely on the basis of deposition of the IO.&rdquo;</p>

<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>High Court&rsquo;s approach</h3><p>Treated the seized articles as an incriminating circumstance the accused had to explain, and relied on the IO&rsquo;s testimony without corroboration.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>Supreme Court&rsquo;s correction</h3><p>The burden of proving a valid seizure was on the prosecution. An unproved seizure generates no circumstance for the accused to answer, and the burden never shifts.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Entrustment and intention — the missing ingredients</h2>
<p>Section 409 IPC requires entrustment and dishonest misappropriation. The original cashbook, daily registers and audit records that would have shown collection and entrustment of the money were never produced, despite the investigating officer&rsquo;s own demands for them — and the trial court had rightly drawn an adverse inference from their absence. A printed list unconnected to the appellant could not fill the gap. On intention, the Court was categorical: there was &ldquo;no evidence whatsoever to indicate any dishonest intention on the part of the appellant.&rdquo;</p>

<h2>The appellate standard at work</h2>
<p>The judgment is a working illustration of the double presumption that protects an acquitted accused: the presumption of innocence, reinforced by a judicial finding of not guilty. A High Court reversing an acquittal must displace both — by showing the trial court&rsquo;s view was not merely debatable but unsustainable. Here the trial court had followed the evidence: hostile panchas, missing primary records, procedural non-compliance. The reversal, built on the IO&rsquo;s uncorroborated word and a shifted burden, could not stand.</p>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>Defence checklist in seizure-based prosecutions:</strong></p>
<p>Test the panchnama against the statutory safeguards for searches; confirm whether the accused signed and received a list of seized articles; track whether the panch witnesses support the seizure; demand the primary records said to establish entrustment; and where the prosecution withholds its best documents, press for the adverse inference the law supplies.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>Under the current procedural code, the discipline is the same: the BNSS carries forward the CrPC&rsquo;s search-witness safeguards, and the principle that procedural irregularity, while not fatal in isolation, counts cumulatively against a prosecution whose core proof is already shaky.</p>
</div>
